{meta title='Whitespace control'}

<p>A template is laid out for people, with line breaks and indentation, and PAD keeps that
layout in what it writes. Usually that does not matter - HTML folds whitespace - but in
plain-text mail, CSV, JSON, console output and inline layouts it does.</p>

<h2>The ~ marker</h2>

<p>A <code>~</code> just inside a brace takes the whitespace on that side of it, newlines
included:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>{open}~tag{close}</code> - the whitespace before the tag</li>
  <li><code>{open}tag~{close}</code> - the whitespace after it, which for an opening tag is the start of its content</li>
  <li><code>{open}~/tag~{close}</code> - both sides of the closing tag</li>
</ul>

<p>Only a brace that opens a tag counts: a <code>~</code> in text, in CSS or in a quoted
parameter stays as it is.</p>

{example 'fragments/whitespace_1'}

<h2>spaceless</h2>

<p>{tag 'spaceless'} takes the whitespace between HTML tags out of everything it wraps, and
at both ends; text inside a tag keeps its spaces. For the whole page there is {tag 'tidy'}.</p>
